    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Creative skills and creative confidence in problem-solving efforts are demanded in all fields, from art, literature to STEM fields. Research shows that art education is strongly connected with creative skills and creative confidence development, but oftentimes students have limited access to creative art resources and personal attention in art curriculum at school (Davies, 2000; White, 2018). Learning opportunities outside of classrooms like drawing apps claim to foster creativity in children. However, most of these apps only focus on teaching artistic techniques, rather than utilizing learning mechanics to reinforce the creative aspect of art. Without quality learning experiences in or outside of school, students in underserved communities are left behind in developing their creativity and confidence. \r\n\r\nNartshell is a browser-based digital game that offers students a safe space to take creative challenges with adaptive difficulty and personalized feedback. Nartshell is a virtual world where students can travel around to learn from game characters that are based on famous artists (e.g., Andy Warhol). Students explore art challenges in order to unlock art tools and cities on the virtual map. They can use the art tools later to create their own paintings. Preliminary tests indicated that learners performed more creatively in art challenges when receiving art-tool rewards, and felt more positive about their accomplishments when receiving constructive feedback. The game can be accessed by any student for free on a website. In future, the game will be introduced to students by collaborating with schools, daycare centers, afterschool programs and local public libraries in underserved communities.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        }
